a  workflow or we have a new team that's  onboarding to the cloud we we spend time  to understand what each specific role  within that business needs to do their  job and we craft and iam role with  exactly the permissions that are  required for that and give it to that  team that allows us to also share those  roles across multiple teams again I  talked about templates and source  control that's really powerful for us  and in the event that that user were  compromised we absolutely know what the  impact of that compromise is based on  what their privileges are so again  custom item roles went to beta a while  back a really powerful tool for us even  over and above the curated roles at  Google now publishers so we talked a lot  about very specific controls things that  we had to address our problem  obviously we only had 15 50 minutes so  they were a lot that we couldn't get  into but some of the quick takeaways in  terms of lessons learned from our  journey and our process as if I haven't  said it enough in this talk it's really  important to collaborate with Google you  know we work very tightly with our  accounting our business manager Tamm  customer engineer are fantastic but we  also work really closely with product  managers and with engineers and again I  can't tell you how empowering it is to  be working on a product and actually be  on the phone way and hang out with the  engineers that are building that product  that that sort of level of iteration  really is what enables quick like  velocity acceleration in the cloud that  that partnership is fundamentals and  again like Google also needs to  understand what your problems are in  order to resolve them and we've actually  seen value not only for our own firm in  some of the controls that I talked about  that evolved through some of our issues  but also everybody else gets to benefit  from them so in the same way as it's  important to contribute to open source  it's important to work with vendors and  help them understand what the problems  are in order to resolve those sandboxing  is interesting again thinking about  velocity it's it's really easy in an  organization like ours with the security  posture that's really tight - just  stifle workflows by not allowing people  to do things but again that's not very  cloud native like how do you enable  people to iterate and experiment with  the freedom that they need but also  maintain your security posture  the answer is difficult right so the  approach that we took is segmenting out  development entirely so we have a  separate G suite domain and a separate  Google cloud platform organization link  to that that's just not accessible from  within two Sigma like you have to be on  our guest network or on an unmanaged  device to access that environment and  what that gives us is the ability to  allow people the freedoms to have the  privileges that they need to iterate and  develop and also protect our IP because  you can't get to our IP from there so  that ultimately led to even better work  for us for us because then when we have  a new team or a team that's considering  a new project  they already really understand what it  is they need from the cloud platform and  I am  and could just tell us that and we could  easily review that approve it and roll  it out again I talked a lot about being  cloud native again there are obvious  security trade-offs here right if you  want the ultimate set of control you'd  build everything on top of Google cloud  engine VMs and then you maintain pretty  much as much as you want but there's a  trade-off there right then you have to  worry about administration you have to  worry about patching you have to worry  about vulnerability management the  trade-off there is to use completely  managed services something like data  prop where you don't have to worry about  any of those things but in turn you lose  that level of transparency in the stack  and you know it's difficult there's no  right or wrong answer every use cases  business risk dependent data dependent  but the key thing as a security team is  to understand what those trade-offs are  and present them to the business so that  the business can make the appropriate  decision based on the value of the  project and the value of the data so  again cloud native controls really are  pushes wherever we can use something  that Google's built they'll support it  develop it and maintain it better than  any custom hacks that we'd have to put  in so that's how preference layer  defenses again I spoke about defense in  depth  extensively so I won't spend too much  time but you don't want to be in a  position where a single compromise or a  mistake or even a failure on the cloud  providers side would lead to you having  a really bad day if your one mistake or  one compromise away from being on the  front page of the newspaper that's a bad  place to be so the more that you can  layer defenses even if it takes more  work even if it's unnecessary I  I like insurance we have an insurance  business or I am in that space and the  last take away from us is is it's  important to move fast again a lot of  organizations that are very security  sensitive have a tendency to take a lot  of time in review and and rightly so the  analysis and getting in-depth but again  you're blocking the business's velocity  and people are going to find ways to  work around you if you take six months  to review one new service so find  workflows and again to borrow quote from  somebody else  Jason Chen who's an engineering director  at Netflix  he said Security's job  should be to build guardrails not gates  so ultimately we're not there yet but my  team's goal is to enable none of our  developers have to think about security  at all we could just build a platform we  can work with our partners Google to  build a platform where developers can  just do their jobs without ever having  to think about security so on that I'd  like to hand back over to offer it to to  wrap up with some final thoughts and  final notes so I hope you got from that  sort of a couple thoughts one is we were  pretty early adopter in this and you you  heard about what we did in that stage  from Jacques and his presentation um  when I got to 2 Sigma just about three  years ago we effectively didn't use the  cloud for much of anything I don't know  maybe a couple of tiny things and we  were fairly able because of jacque's and  a few others work to get to a situation  where maybe half of our compute load was  sort of on the cloud although we did it  in ways that were not as cloud native as  we would like and we were not able to  fully use all the services that we knew  that were coming as we got to the second  phase of this working more closely with  Google we were able to use standard  technologies after certainly providing  input as to what they should be but able  to use those and now we've gotten to  that stage where not only are we doing a  lot of compute in the cloud we're doing  a lot more heterogeneous computing in  the cloud using a lot more different  services and we do think that's the  future of all this is that I don't think  any of us unless maybe we're Google or a  very very large company can build all  the things that that that we will want  to use they're just too big  we can't can't build bigquery or  something like that it's a huge project  to go do so we're very excited about the  future we think that Google now  understands a lot of these principles of  that really come from the notion that  ensuring that data is not universally  accessible and useful that's not the  only thing right you need to be able to  control that access and you need to be  able to  roll it in a way as Nick said very  eloquently that you're not one mistake  or one attack away from being on the  front page of the New York Times as it  has been said so we certainly don't we  we think that seems to now be the case  and we're very excited about being able  to rapidly move forward in a lot more  fronts these are the talks that we're  giving so on the security I guess it's  already kind of late on Tuesday so  without a time machine you can't go back  to the earlier talks that we do not have  if we did that would be quite  interesting in our business and in on  Wednesday and Thursday you'll see the  talks though that are listed in  insecurity and as I mentioned the  serverless computing talk the halite  talk I think is at the same time slot  right it's also at 4:30 5:00 in the  afternoon if you want to have some fun  and see how maybe IAI will continue to  be explored in ways that are really  challenging so we should open this up to  some questions after the obligatory  we're hiring we do exciting things as  you can tell we're trying to be on the  forefront more than any other firm that  we know if we get to answer the question  with all of the security and computation  what is the effect of machine learning  on AI in the entire world of economic  optimization no one knows the answer to  that question in my opinion I've been in  this field leading research teams for  many decades we know small areas where  it's useful we don't know the breadth so  the firm is dedicated to some really  interesting problems that have solved  will make economies in the world I think  far more efficient and make our firm  very successful so thank you very much  for your time let's take some questions  if there are some  [Music]

